so let's get started, shall we?

109
00:07:47,000 --> 00:08:01,000
I want to tell you about Richard Francis Thurman.

110
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
He was always known as John John Thurman.

111
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
He was born on the 4th of April 1911,

112
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
and passed away in 1985,

113
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
but he was the third camp chief at Gilwell Park in 1943.

114
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
He was only 32 years old when he became the camp chief at Gilwell Park in 1943,

115
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
and he remained in that position for 26 years.

116
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
He was the author of 15 books on scouting

117
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
and he made a considerable impact on world scouting,

118
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and his influence lives on through his books

119
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and through his work in wood badge training.

120
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Now, one of my recurring themes here is that the more things change,

121
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
the more they remain the same,

122
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
or everything old is new again

123
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
because you know, after a century

124
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
there's not a whole lot we're going to run into in scouting

125
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
that hasn't been run into before.

126
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Most of the difficulties and challenges that we encounter as scouters

127
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
are old ground. okay,

128
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
There's a lot of people who've already dealt with them and overcome them,

129
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
and I like to look back and to see how they did that,

130
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
because it's a way to form our own resolutions

131
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
to some of these difficulties

132
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and a way to understand the really important basic ideas of scouting.

133
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
And one of those basic ideas is the patrol method,

134
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
and John Thurman wrote the Patrol Leaders Handbook in 1950.

135
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
So pay attention to that date for a moment. 1950,

136
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
the good old days when everything was great, right

137
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
And scouts were scouts and well you know, I'm going to tell you

138
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
They were encountering the same kind of challenges then.

139
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
None of this is new

140
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:41,000
and that makes what Thurman had to write timeless advice

141
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
that is meaningful for us.

142
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
It's easy to get a copy of most of his books in PDF format

143
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and I'll link to resources about that in the podcast notes. okay,

144
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:57,000
But I want to read from his introduction to this Patrol Leaders Handbook,

145
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
because it is one of the better explanations of patrol method

146
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
that you're likely to encounter

147
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
and really get you into the spirit of what the patrol method is about.

148
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
So Thurman wrote:

149
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
the patrol system is made up of a lot of littles.

150
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
This is why there are difficulties about it

151
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and why there are problems,

152
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
why it's not too easy to understand

153
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
and why I hope from this book

154
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
you'll get a real picture and a vision

155
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:29,000
of all that the patrol system can and should be mean and do.

156
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
I've always been glad it isn't too easy.

157
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
If all we had to do was write patrol system

158
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
over the entrance to every true headquarters

159
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and sort of a miracle resulted,

160
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
it would really be too simple to bother with.

161
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
But fortunately- and I mean fortunately-

162
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
it isn't as easy as that.

163
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
It does not get any easier as years go by,

164
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and perhaps in that lies its secret,

165
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
its charm and its possibilities.

166
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
It always needs, and always will need,

167
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
two special qualities:

168
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
the one common sense, the other effort.

169
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
I hope you have the first,

170
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
which is by no means as common as it should be,

171
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
and will make the second,

172
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
because upon these two things

173
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
the rest will depend.

174
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
I, and indeed many others, can explain a little,

175
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
suggest a lot, advise perhaps

176
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
and encourage always,

177
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
but no one except you, the patrol leader

178
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
can really do anything about the patrol system,

179
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
because it really does all depend upon you.

180
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,000
It is your show and it is always up to you.

181
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
When scouting was started,

182
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
the idea of the patrol system was really

183
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
a very revolutionary thing.

184
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
In fact, a lot of people criticized it

185
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
and told Baden Powell that it was dangerous,

186
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
that it would not work

187
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and that he was asking for trouble

188
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and that the boys would let him down.

189
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Things have gone by.

190
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
the method of the patrol system

191
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
has carried far outside scouting.

192
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
In the early days of scouting,

193
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
boys from all over the country,

194
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and later from all over the world,

195
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
bought the book Scouting for Boys

196
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and formed themselves into patrols.

197
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
I want to repeat:

198
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
formed themselves.

199
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
This means that someone,

200
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
a leader not appointed by anyone in particular

201
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
but chosen by other fellows

202
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
because those same other fellows

203
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
were willing to follow him,

204
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
a crowd of chaps who wanted to be scouts.

205
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
They formed a patrol

206
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and they started to train themselves

207
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
using the book Scouting for Boys

208
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
as the only guide.

209
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
But they found- as patrols have always found-

210
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
that there were many things they did not know,

211
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
many things they could not find out

212
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
and many things they could not do

213
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
without the help of some adult,

214
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And so the practice grew

215
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
of a number of patrols getting together,

216
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
forming a troop and usually finding

217
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
their own scout master.

218
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Sometime in the year 1908

219
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
that a gang of boys saved up their pennies

220
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
and managed to purchase two or three copies

221
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
of Scouting for Boys,

222
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
They met together in an old barn

223
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
and tried many of the things

224
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
that Baden Powell suggested they should do.

225
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
They had a lot of fun

226
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
and learned a great deal,

227
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and they got into quite a few scrapes.

228
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
One day they came across the chapter on camping.

229
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Now, as they had tried all the things suggested,

230
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
they were not going to be defeated

231
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
by one, which seemed a little more difficult.

232
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Of course, none of them had ever been to camp

233
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
and they didn't know the first thing about it,

234
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
but they had a real spirit of adventure.

235
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
They were prepared to try anything,

236
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
not only once but until they got it right.

237
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
And so they talked it all over

238
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and devised a manner of means

239
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
to get things they thought would be suitable.

240
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Bill, Jack and Tom were to get the food,

241
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
just how nobody told them.

242
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Martin, the leader, was going to get the tent,

243
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
and Alec the Youngest said

244
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
he thought he could get a cart.

245
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
The leader also said he would look after the cooking pots,

246
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and each boy was made responsible

247
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
for getting his own bedding.

248
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
So it happened that one Saturday afternoon, early in June,

249
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
they duly assembled at the barn

250
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and started out on their travels.

251
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
No questions were asked,

252
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
although young Alec did indicate

253
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
it had been very difficult to get the toy cart

254
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
away from his baby brother,

255
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
and Jack was not very complimentary

256
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
about the very old tarpaulin

257
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
that the patrol leader said was to be a tent.

258
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
but they were all amazed and delighted

259
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
at the quantity of food that had been produced,

260
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
which by present day standards

261
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
would have been enough for a month.

262
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Well, they packed what they could into the cart,

263
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and after the food was in there

264
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
there was not really much room for anything else,

265
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
so the rest was carried.

266
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
They did not know where they were going.

267
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
they had no map and I doubt

268
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
if they could have read one. in any case,

269
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
They set off literally into the blue,

270
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
out of the village and over the hill

271
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
in the west country, because

272
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
they were not the sort of fellows

273
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
who walked on roads if they could avoid them.

274
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
but all their preparations had taken a long time

275
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and it was quite late in the evening

276
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
when they settled on a place to camp.

277
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
Pleasant enough campsite between a by-road

278
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
and a stream.

279
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
They decided the stream would do

280
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
for washing water and for cooking.

281
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
It was all very unhygienic,

282
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
but they did remain alive for many years.

283
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
to tell the tale,

284
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
They lit a fire, as they had already learned to do,

285
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and used more than two matches,

286
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and it was a very large fire.

287
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
They then set to

288
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and cook the food.

289
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
They mixed some very queer concoctions

290
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
and they burned quite a lot,

291
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
but the food had never tasted better

292
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
to those particular boys.

293
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Then, rather late, they tried to put up the tent.

294
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
The leader had a knife

295
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and cut down two saplings and somehow,

296
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
with string and ingenuity,

297
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
they rigged up the tarpaulin into some sort of tent

298
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
and as it became very dark,

299
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
very tired but very happy,

300
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
they crept into the tent

301
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
and got into bed.

302
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
Fortunately, as they didn't know anything about ground sheets,

303
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
it was very dry.

304
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
They did not know that cold rises from the ground.

305
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
in fact they did not know very much at all,

306
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
but they were learning by doing.

307
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
They talked in the tent

308
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
feeling very much like pioneers

309
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
and gradually, one by one, dropped off to sleep,

310
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
all except the leader

311
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
who felt a special responsibility

312
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
and although he had not told the others,

313
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
he was going to try to keep awake,

314
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Suddenly, in what seemed to him

315
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
to be in the middle of the night-

316
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
but was actually only 10 o'clock-

317
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
he heard footsteps, which stopped

318
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
outside the tent.

319
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
The leader kept very quiet until the light

320
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
of a torch shone

321
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
and he looked out to see a very large pair of boots.

322
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
As his eye traveled upwards,

323
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
he saw a pair of dark blue trousers

324
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
and, from somewhere

325
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
a very long way above,

326
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
a very deep voice said:

327
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and what are you supposed to be doing?

328
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
The others woke

329
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
and there, to their dismay, was a policeman.

330
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
The leader said:

331
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
we are scouts and we're camping,

332
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
but in 1908 not many policemen

333
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
had heard about scouts,

334
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
and certainly this one hadn't.

335
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and his answer was:

336
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
well, you are coming home now.

337
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Very sorrowfully, the patrol

338
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
packed up their gear and went with the policemen.

339
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
They were very surprised to find

340
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
that they had not far to go

341
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
owing to having no map

342
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
and practically no sense of direction.

343
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
they had almost traveled

344
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
in a circle and were in fact

345
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
camping quite near home.

346
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
We will draw a veil over what happened

347
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
when they got to their various homes,

348
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
but they were not daunted,

349
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
as they had arranged to meet at their barn

350
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
the following night. The Sunday evening

351
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
found them together again in the barn,

352
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
and the leader had not been wasting his time.

353
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
He had reread

354
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
scouting for boys

355
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and the first thing he said was:

356
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
look here, chaps.

357
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
I have been reading the book again

358
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and it talks about a scout master.

359
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
I think that is what we want.

360
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
he would have kept the policemen away.

361
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
well, that is one of the jobs

362
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
of a scout master and it is one of the reasons

363
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
why your patrol or troop needs one,

364
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
somebody who will arrange

365
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
for you to carry out scouting

366
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
without fear of being erupted.

367
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
I do want you to remember

368
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
that this patrol started on its own.

369
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
they were out together and they came to realize

370
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
the kind of things that a scout master

371
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
can do for the patrol,

372
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
and they found a scout master for themselves.

373
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
that troop is still running,

374
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
perhaps because it started in the right spirit.

375
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
I suppose today it is usual

376
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
for the scout masters to form the patrol

377
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
and then the scouts to get on with it.

378
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
the important thing about the old method

379
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
was that right from the start

380
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
the patrol leader realized

381
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
he was really the key man in the whole business

382
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
and if he failed, then his patrol.

383
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
he did not lean on the scout master,

384
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
except for those things

385
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
where he was quite naturally lacking in knowledge

386
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
or ability and had to go to an adult

387
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
for help.

388
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
what I am afraid happens so often now

389
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
is that patrols are not really patrols at all,

390
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
but only used as convenient

391
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
divisions for a scout troop.

392
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
a scout troop ought to be a

393
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
meeting together of patrols.

394
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
if a patrol is only

395
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
a convenient division,

396
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
it is not a patrol at all.

397
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
a troop meeting ought to be a place

398
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
where patrols meet together

399
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
to compete against each other

400
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
and to encourage each other

401
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and, having met and learned all they can,

402
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
should go away as patrols

403
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
to practice and get more experience.

404
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
unfortunately, what has happened in this country,

405
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
in many parts of the world,

406
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
is that the troop meets too often

407
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
and the patrol not enough.

408
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
I hope your patrol is going to be different,

409
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
that it is really going to meet

410
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
as a patrol, away from the troop,

411
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
without the scouters

412
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
going back to the scouters for advice and help.

413
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
the wise scout master knows

414
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
that this is his job, but sometimes

415
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
the patrol leader does not let the scout master

416
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
do his proper job

417
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
but will insist on leaning on him for everything.

418
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
I want to remind you of one of the things

419
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
that Baden Powell said about the patrol.

420
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
he said: expect a great deal

421
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
of your patrol leaders

422
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
and 9 times out of 10 they will play

423
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
up to your expectations.

424
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
but if you are always going to nurse them

425
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
to trust them to do things well,

426
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
you will never get them to do things

427
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
on their own initiative.

428
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
so that's just a part of

429
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
the introduction to John Thurman's

430
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
1950

431
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
patrol leaders handbook,

432
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
and a lot of that sounds like it could be said today

433
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
and could have been said 10 years ago

434
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and 20 and 30 and 40 years ago.

435
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
this is a recurring thing.

436
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
the whole patrol system idea is not easy

437
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
to get. remember what he said.

438
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
it's pretty difficult and it should be so

439
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
and he's thankful that it is.

440
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
patrols need to be not just

441
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
convenient subdivisions of troops

442
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
but actual vibrant

443
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
living things.

444
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
they are really the basis of scouting

445
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
and a lot of people get back to me when I talk like this

446
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
and they say: well, man, this is really frustrating

447
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
because boys just don't know

448
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
how to do this anymore.

449
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
well, they do. the problem is

450
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
is we don't have the confidence

451
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
to let them figure it out

452
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
and remember this is supposed to be

453
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
difficult, this is supposed to be

454
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
hard, this is supposed to be a challenge

455
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
and it's as challenging for scouts

456
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
as it is for adults.

457
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
this is as much of a foreign idea to them

458
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
as it is to you, but I find a lot

459
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
of encouragement in the idea

460
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
that 66 years ago,

461
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
when John Thurman wrote this,

462
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
he could have been writing it into answers to emails

463
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
that I receive all the time.

464
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
I leave you by repeating one of the first

465
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
things he said in this introduction,

466
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
and that is:

467
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
this patrol system takes two things:

468
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
common sense and effort.

469
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
it's not easy, although it can be

470
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
daunting, and it can be a little

471
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
confusing and difficult to see

472
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
the initiative happening sometimes.

473
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
use your common sense

474
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
and keep extending effort in that direction.

475
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
you're going to be happy and you'll

476
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
be rewarded by seeing the patrol

477
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
system grow. but of course

478
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
that is secondary to the

479
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
rewards your scouts will derive

480
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
from a really active and vibrant

481
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
application of the patrol system.

482
